Magnetized winds of rotating stars result in perpendicular termination shocks that can accelerate electrons (Xu et al., 2019) but not protons (Caprioli and Spitkovsky, 2014).

While moving through the ISM, the stellar wind bubble piles up a magnetic draping layer at the contact discontinuity. Accelerated electrons diffuse onto these field lines and escape from their acceleration site. Subsequently, they move away from the star, emit synchrotron radiation in the strongly magnetized flux tubes of the ISM, and illuminate the NTFs (see left panel of Fig. 3).

